import{_ as s,C as o,o as n,c as i,ag as t,E as r}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Edges","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"agentic/edges.md","filePath":"agentic/edges.md"}'),d={name:"agentic/edges.md"};function h(l,e,c,g,p,u){const a=o("EdgesFigure");return n(),i("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<h1 id="edges" tabindex="-1">Edges <a class="header-anchor" href="#edges" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Edges&quot;">​</a></h1><p>An edge is an input at the border of what a part does: a <code>NaN</code>, an empty list, a path with a space, the other floating-point precision. A spec that does not decide an edge leaves the answer to the builder, and a critic that probes the edge finds the gap as a blocking defect. With several open edges, each round of the loop finds one more, and the loop does not close. The harness therefore keeps a catalogue of the edges that critics found, by the kind of part, and the planner decides each one before the build. This page describes the catalogue and how it grows.</p><h2 id="the-edge-catalogue" tabindex="-1">The edge catalogue <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-edge-catalogue" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The edge catalogue&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The catalogue is one file of the harness, <code>skills/build-part/edges.md</code>. It has about 260 entries, one bullet each, in seven sections, one for each kind of part:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">kind of part</th><th style="text-align:right;">entries</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">a part that predicts what another tool reads or does</td><td style="text-align:right;">28</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">a numerical method</td><td style="text-align:right;">44</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">a type or an interface</td><td style="text-align:right;">33</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">a tool that reads files or a tree</td><td style="text-align:right;">61</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">a step that runs a user&#39;s script</td><td style="text-align:right;">15</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">a lint cleanup</td><td style="text-align:right;">4</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">a migration of tests or of text</td><td style="text-align:right;">75</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An entry names the class of input. Most entries also give the mechanism of the failure and the remedy, and a new entry names the part that found it. Two short entries of the section for a numerical method:</p><div class="language-markdown vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">markdown</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> a subnormal input; a result that underflows to zero; an anchor value near </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">`floatmin`</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> a tie that round-off decides, for example a safeguard of the form </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">`w &lt;= wprev / 2`</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The first section is a design rule more than a list. A guard or a renderer predicts what another program reads, and each gap between the prediction and the program is a defect. Its first entry says: fail closed or emit a canonical form, never emulate the other program.</p><h2 id="the-cycle" tabindex="-1">The cycle <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-cycle" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The cycle&quot;">​</a></h2>',9)),r(a),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<p><em>How the catalogue grows.</em> The planner writes the spec&#39;s <em>Decided at the edges</em> from the catalogue. The builder builds to the spec, and the critic probes every edge of the part&#39;s kind that the spec does not decide. A defect outside every clause of the spec, in a class that the catalogue does not hold, becomes a new entry after the loop, and the next spec of that kind decides it. The critic also reads the catalogue directly.</p><p>Each role uses the catalogue:</p><ul><li><strong>The planner</strong> writes <em>Decided at the edges</em> from the section of the part&#39;s kind: one line per edge, with the answer. An edge that it cannot decide makes the part a <code>decision</code> part, which waits for you (<a href="./../components/skills#plan-parts"><code>plan-parts</code></a>).</li><li><strong>The spec gate</strong> refuses a <code>build</code> part in <code>Packages/</code> or <code>Experiments/</code> whose sections lack <em>Decided at the edges</em> or <em>Tests catch</em>. A spec in the spec-delta format must pass <code>spec-validate.py</code> instead, and there each decided edge is a scenario of the delta (<a href="./specs-and-gates#the-gates">Specs and gates</a>).</li><li><strong>The critic</strong> of round 1 probes every edge of the part&#39;s kind that the section does not decide, then inputs that the builder did not try.</li><li><strong>A fix builder</strong> that adds a guard, an exit or a status reads the section first, and tests its new rule on each class of edge that the rule can reach. A fix for one reproducer changes the result on other inputs too.</li><li><strong>The arbitrator</strong> reads it when a loop does not converge, because a run of defects of one class points at an edge that nobody decided.</li></ul><h2 id="how-an-entry-is-added" tabindex="-1">How an entry is added <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-an-entry-is-added" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How an entry is added&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each clause of a spec has a <em>domain</em>: the finite set of inputs that it names, such as the decided edges or the named mutants. A failure inside the domain fails the clause and blocks the round. A failure outside every domain is an other defect of the kind <em>found late</em>: the critic records it with its reproducer, and it does not block. A verify round also records a defect outside the fix diff as found late. A found-late defect shows an input class that the spec did not reach.</p><p>After the loop, <code>build-part</code> records each class of a found-late defect that the catalogue does not hold, as one line in the section of the part&#39;s kind, with the part that found it. <code>build-reviewed</code> adds each class of defect that its critic found and that the catalogue does not hold. The line goes into the source of the catalogue in the harness, with a changelog fragment, and the next <code>harness install --apply</code> installs it.</p><h2 id="the-limits" tabindex="-1">The limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The catalogue only grows.</strong> No rule removes an entry yet. <strong>Planned:</strong> an entry goes when a deterministic check catches its class, or when it duplicates another entry. A class that a <a href="./static-checks">static check</a> catches no longer needs a line that the planner reads.</li><li><strong>An entry names a class, not a test.</strong> The planner still decides what the code does at the edge, and the builder still writes the test.</li><li><strong>The catalogue is written for the packages of one tree.</strong> Its entries name packages by their role, such as &quot;the integrator package&quot;, so the text stays true when a package is renamed.</li></ul><p><strong>In another language.</strong> The sections are by the kind of part, not by the language. The sections of a tool that reads files and of a migration apply to any language; the section of a numerical method applies wherever floating point does.</p>',9))])}const y=s(d,[["render",h]]);export{m as __pageData,y as default};
